#{[17592186237846 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 6+(-1)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-3)}{\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{b}\\cdot \\mathbf{a}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}-{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b} & =\\frac{4}{7}-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{7}-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{3}{21}-\\frac{7}{21}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186237847 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-7)+\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}+3\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{(-7)^{2}+{\\sqrt{6}}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{64}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{p}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{p}_{x}^{2}+{p}_{y}^{2}+{p}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{\\sqrt{6}}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}+{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{p}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q} & =1+2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186237924 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x \\\\ y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-5\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=y+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${x}^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=1.$"] [17592186237925 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x-3}{5} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y+2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{x-3}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y+2}{3}\\right)}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\left(\\frac{x-3}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y+2}{3}\\right)}^{2}=1.\n$$"] [17592186237926 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=x \\\\ y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t & =1 \\\\ {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t & =1-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}t & =\\sqrt{1-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, using the definition of a tangent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}t=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}}{\\operatorname{cos}t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{x}.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{x}.$"] [17592186238016 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2x & 3 & 2 \\\\ x & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 2 \\\\ x & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 3 \\\\ x & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(-3-2)\\mathbf{i}-(-2x-2x)\\mathbf{j}+(2x-3x)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-5,-4x,x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-4x)^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+16{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17{x}^{2}+25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{93} \\\\ \\sqrt{17{x}^{2}+25} & =\\sqrt{93} \\\\ 17{x}^{2}+25 & =93 \\\\ 17{x}^{2} & =68 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm 2.$"] [17592186238017 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2x\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2x\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(4x-0)\\mathbf{i}-(-6x-0)\\mathbf{j}+(12-4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4x,6x,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{(4x)^{2}+(6x)^{2}+(8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+36{x}^{2}+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{52{x}^{2}+64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{77} \\\\ \\sqrt{52{x}^{2}+64} & =\\sqrt{77} \\\\ 52{x}^{2}+64 & =77 \\\\ 52{x}^{2} & =13 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.$"] [17592186238018 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & 2x & 3 \\\\ 2 & -x & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 3 \\\\ -x & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2x \\\\ 2 & -x\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2x+3x)\\mathbf{i}-(-1-6)\\mathbf{j}+(x-4x)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨5x,7,-3x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{(5x)^{2}+(7)^{2}+(-3x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{x}^{2}+49+9{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34{x}^{2}+49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{355} \\\\ \\sqrt{34{x}^{2}+49} & =\\sqrt{355} \\\\ 34{x}^{2}+49 & =355 \\\\ 34{x}^{2} & =306 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm 3.$"] [17592186238021 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 4 & x & 3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}x & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & x \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0-9)\\mathbf{i}-(0+6)\\mathbf{j}+(12+2x)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-9,-6,12+2x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{(-9)^{2}+(-6)^{2}+(12+2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81+36+144+48x+4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{261+48x+4{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{181} \\\\ \\sqrt{261+48x+4{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{181} \\\\ 261+48x+4{x}^{2} & =181 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+48x+80 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+12x+20 & =0 \\\\ (x+10)(x+2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-10,\\,-2.$"] [17592186238022 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -x & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -x & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -x\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(6+4x)\\mathbf{i}-(0+4)\\mathbf{j}+(0+3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨6+4x,-4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{(6+4x)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+48x+16{x}^{2}+16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{61+48x+16{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{221} \\\\ \\sqrt{61+48x+16{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{221} \\\\ 61+48x+16{x}^{2} & =221 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}+48x-160 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x-10 & =0 \\\\ (x+5)(x-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-5,\\,2.$"] [17592186238024 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2x\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2x\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(4x-3)\\mathbf{i}-(0+6)\\mathbf{j}+(0-4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4x-3,-6,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{(4x-3)^{2}+(-6)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-24x+9+36+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-24x+61}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{53} \\\\ \\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-24x+61} & =\\sqrt{53} \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-24x+61 & =53 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-24x+8 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3x+1 & =0 \\\\ (2x-1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2},\\hspace{0.167em}1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,1.$"] [17592186240865 "A vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{F}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}.\n$$"] [17592186240866 "A vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{F}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$"] [17592186240867 "A vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}(4x-3y))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}(4x-3y))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}(4x-3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3\\operatorname{cos}(4x-3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{F}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=4\\operatorname{cos}(4x-3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3\\operatorname{cos}(4x-3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$"] [17592186240870 "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ be a vector field with domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf{F}$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ we have $P=ay-2x{y}^{3}$ and $Q=x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=a-6x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1-6x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-6x{y}^{2} & =1-6x{y}^{2} \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=1.$"] [17592186240872 "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ be a vector field with domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf{F}$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ we have $P=\\frac{axy}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{ax}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{ax}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ ax & =2x \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=2.$"] [17592186240905 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,2t⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(\\mathbf{r}(\\pi ))-f(\\mathbf{r}(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2\\pi )-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}2\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}0+\\operatorname{cos}0) \\\\  & =(0+1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186240906 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(\\mathbf{r}(\\pi ))-f(\\mathbf{r}(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}\\cdot 2-{2}^{2}\\cdot \\pi )-({0}^{2}\\cdot 0-{0}^{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =(2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186240907 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,{t}^{3}+1⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,2)$ and $(2,9).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(\\mathbf{r}(2))-f(\\mathbf{r}(1)) \\\\  & =f(2,9)-f(1,2) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{9\\pi }{2}\\right)-{e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot 1-{e}^{1}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186240908 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,2{t}^{2}⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [-1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨-1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(-1,2)$ and $(2,8).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(\\mathbf{r}(2))-f(\\mathbf{r}(-1)) \\\\  & =f(2,8)-f(-1,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}+{8}^{3})-\\frac{1}{3}((-1)^{3}+{2}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(520)-\\frac{1}{3}(7) \\\\  & =\\frac{520}{3}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =171.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186240909 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(2\\pi ) & =⟨1,0,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0,0)$ and $(1,0,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(\\mathbf{r}(2\\pi ))-f(\\mathbf{r}(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,0,2\\pi )-f(1,0,0) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot 2\\pi +1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}0)-({1}^{2}\\cdot 0+1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}0) \\\\  & =(2\\pi +0)-0 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186240918 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ Since we're given that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(1,e).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(1,e)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =({1}^{3}\\cdot {e}^{3}+{1}^{2}-2\\cdot {e}^{2})-({0}^{3}\\cdot {1}^{3}+{0}^{2}-2\\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{3}+1-2{e}^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={e}^{3}-2{e}^{2}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186240919 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ Since we're given that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(2\\pi ) & =⟨4{\\pi }^{2}+1,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $(4{\\pi }^{2}+1,2\\pi ).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(4{\\pi }^{2}+1,2\\pi )-f(1,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}+1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}2\\pi -{e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}0 \\\\  & ={e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}+1}-e \\\\  & =e({e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186240920 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ Since we're given that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2xy-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}y-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-1⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(4,3).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(4,3)-f(0,-1) \\\\  & =({4}^{2}\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 3)-({0}^{2}\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =48-12 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186240921 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ Since we're given that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{ln}2,4)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (3\\operatorname{ln}2)^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}-3\\operatorname{ln}2\\cdot 4)-(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3}-0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =9{\\operatorname{ln}}^{3}2+\\frac{64}{3}-12\\operatorname{ln}2-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =9{\\operatorname{ln}}^{3}2-12\\operatorname{ln}2+21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186240924 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\underbrace{{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}x}_{P(x,y)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\underbrace{(2y\\operatorname{sin}x+{e}^{y})}_{Q(x,y)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ Notice that the domain of $\\mathbf{F}$ is ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\pi +{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}})-({0}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}0+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =(0+{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}})-(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186240925 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\underbrace{({e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}x)}_{P(x,y)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\underbrace{(x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}x)}_{Q(x,y)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ Notice that the domain of $\\mathbf{F}$ is ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,t+\\operatorname{cos}t⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,\\pi -1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(\\pi ,\\pi -1).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,\\pi -1)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(\\pi \\cdot {e}^{\\pi -1}+(\\pi -1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\pi )-(0\\cdot {e}^{1}+1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}0) \\\\  & =(\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}+0)-0 \\\\  & =\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186240926 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\underbrace{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}y}_{P(x,y)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\underbrace{\\operatorname{cos}y\\operatorname{sin}x}_{Q(x,y)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ Notice that the domain of $\\mathbf{F}$ is ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}t}+\\operatorname{cos}t⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,2⟩, \\\\ r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},e).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},e)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}e)-(\\operatorname{sin}0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}e-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186240928 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\underbrace{2x{e}^{-y}}_{P(x,y)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\underbrace{(2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y})}_{Q(x,y)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ Notice that the domain of $\\mathbf{F}$ is ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x{e}^{-y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x{e}^{-y}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{-y}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}(t-1)⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [2,3].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨2,0⟩, \\\\ r(3) & =⟨3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(2,0)$ and $(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(3,\\operatorname{ln}2)-f(2,0) \\\\  & =({3}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}2}+(\\operatorname{ln}2)^{2})-({2}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-0}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}+{\\operatorname{ln}}^{2}2-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+{\\operatorname{ln}}^{2}2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186240929 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\underbrace{(6{x}^{2}-y)}_{P(x,y)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\underbrace{(-x)}_{Q(x,y)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ Notice that the domain of $\\mathbf{F}$ is ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =6{x}^{2}-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{3}-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}-{e}^{\\sqrt{t+1}}⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-e⟩, \\\\ r(3) & =⟨3,9-{e}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-e)$ and $(3,9-{e}^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(3,9-{e}^{2})-f(0,e) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {3}^{3}-3\\cdot (9-{e}^{2}))-(2\\cdot {0}^{3}-0\\cdot (-e)) \\\\  & =(54-27+3{e}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =(27+3{e}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =3({e}^{2}+9).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186241078 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{sin}x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}y) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{cos}x-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}y\\operatorname{sin}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186241107 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\times \\mathbf{F}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 3{z}^{2} & 2y & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y)]\\mathbf{i}-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{z}^{2})]\\mathbf{j}+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{z}^{2})]\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0-0)\\mathbf{i}-(0-6z)\\mathbf{j}+(0-0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =6z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186241111 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\times \\mathbf{F}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ {e}^{y+z} & 1 & xyz\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(1)]\\mathbf{i}-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{y+z})]\\mathbf{j}+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y+z})]\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(xz-0)\\mathbf{i}-(yz-{e}^{y+z})\\mathbf{j}+(0-{e}^{y+z})\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({e}^{y+z}-yz)\\mathbf{j}-{e}^{y+z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(2,1,-1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F})(2,1,-1) & =2(-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({e}^{1+(-1)}-1(-1))\\mathbf{j}-{e}^{1+(-1)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186241157 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(x+1) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{9}{(x+1)}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2 & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(y+2) & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{9}{(y+2)}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9}{(x+1)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{(y+2)}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ 4{(x+1)}^{2}+4{(y+2)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation of the circle is ${(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}.$"] [17592186241158 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) & =5\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+\\frac{1}{2}) & =5\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(y+\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $$\n\\displaystyle {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25.\n$$"] [17592186243923 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\frac{25}{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}.$"] [17592186244233 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/5f/5f34ac74599937e0ae181126ff9a3da5b29dfe213614a1f23ca10c7062f22f7d.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}(3xy-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3xy-1$ and $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3xy-1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6x-3x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}3x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x[y]_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x(2x-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}-3{x}^{3})\\,dx \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2(2)^{3}-\\frac{3(2)^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =16-12 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186244238 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/57/574172dda3031b9bb44dbcfb46265453ae543a75bccf8db24e93625e7154bba9.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+({e}^{y}+{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{x}-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+{x}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\hspace{0.167em}-1\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({e}^{y}+{x}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2xy+{y}^{2}]}_{-1}^{1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2x(1)+(1)^{2}-2x(-1)-(-1)^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}4x\\,dx \\\\  & =2{[{x}^{2}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2[(1)^{2}-(-1)^{2}] \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186244245 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/fa/fafd281fc63fb276c9759fd84f722096d494aa3e3fe9441f43aa6ecd1f9dfda9.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}(2x+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+({x}^{2}+2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x+{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}/3}(2x-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[2xy-{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{{x}^{2}/3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{9})dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{45}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)^{4}}{6}-\\frac{(3)^{5}}{45} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{27}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186244257 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/e3/e32b8e7d6ce80d7cedad928f9a2f7acc3c3d7f06add7909e856db27d154068d6.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{ln}(2+x)-{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247030 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}2-3u,\\hspace{0.222em}1+u-v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2-3u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1+u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-3,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2-3u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1+u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}3⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's denote\n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le u\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}-1\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nThen, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{22}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{22}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{22}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247031 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3}⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}2v,\\hspace{0.222em}2{v}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 2{v}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 2v & 2{v}^{2}\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2{v}^{2}\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2v⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2v⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-2{v}^{2})^{2}+(2v)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{4}+4{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}({v}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =2v\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sqrt{{v}^{2}}=v$ since $v\\ge 0$ on $D.$\n\nNext, let's denote\n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le u\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le v\\le \\sqrt{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nThen, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2v\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2v\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}2v\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}u\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}2v\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nw={v}^{2}+1\\,⇒\\,\\text{d}w=2v\\,\\text{d}v,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}2v\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{w}\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{w}^{3}}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\sqrt{{4}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(8-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247040 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}uv,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}u,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2u & v & 0 \\\\ 0 & u & v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}v & 0 \\\\ u & v\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & v\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}2u & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-2uv,\\hspace{0.222em}2{u}^{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-2uv,\\hspace{0.222em}2{u}^{2}⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({v}^{2})^{2}+(-2uv)^{2}+(2{u}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+4{u}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({v}^{2}+2{u}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & ={v}^{2}+2{u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's denote\n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le u\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nThen, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({v}^{2}+2{u}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({v}^{2}+2{u}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[u{v}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({v}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3})\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}+\\frac{2}{3}v]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{2}^{3}+2(2)-({1}^{3}+2(1))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(9) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247046 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that the region ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 9$ corresponds to the disk\n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 9\\},\n$$\n\nof radius $3.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}) \\\\  & =9\\pi \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247058 "For a surface with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}.$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2u \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2u \\\\ 1 & -2v\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2u \\\\ 0 & -2v\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2u,\\hspace{0.222em}2v,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247059 "For a surface with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}.$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u-{e}^{v}⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-{e}^{v})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-{e}^{v})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-{e}^{v}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -{e}^{v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -{e}^{v}\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{v}\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{v},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247060 "For a surface with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}.$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2}+1,\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{3}+1,\\hspace{0.222em}2u+v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}+1),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{3}+1),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{3}+1),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}3{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2u & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3{v}^{2} & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3{v}^{2} & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3{v}^{2}\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-2u,\\hspace{0.222em}6u{v}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247065 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}v⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}-{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}v & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}v \\\\ 0 & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}v & -{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}v & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}v \\\\ {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}v & -{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}v\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}v \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}v\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}v \\\\ 0 & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{2u}({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}v+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}v),\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{2u},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}v⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨-{e}^{2u},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}v⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-{e}^{2u})^{2}+({e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}v)^{2}+({e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}v)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{4u}+{e}^{2u}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}v+{e}^{2u}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}v} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{4u}+{e}^{2u}({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}v+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}v)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{4u}+{e}^{2u}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}({e}^{2u}+1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}}\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}+1} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{\\color{blue}{e}^{u}\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}+1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247071 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u\\operatorname{sin}v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u\\operatorname{cos}v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\hspace{0.222em}2\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{cos}v⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u\\operatorname{sin}v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u\\operatorname{cos}v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}2u\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}-u\\operatorname{sin}v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2u & 2\\operatorname{sin}v & \\operatorname{cos}v \\\\ 0 & 2u\\operatorname{cos}v & -u\\operatorname{sin}v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}v & \\operatorname{cos}v \\\\ 2u\\operatorname{cos}v & -u\\operatorname{sin}v\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}2u & \\operatorname{cos}v \\\\ 0 & -u\\operatorname{sin}v\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2\\operatorname{sin}v \\\\ 0 & 2u\\operatorname{cos}v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2u{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}v-2u{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}v,\\hspace{0.222em}2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}4{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2u,\\hspace{0.222em}2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}4{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}v⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨-2u,\\hspace{0.222em}2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}4{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}v⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2u)^{2}+(2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}v)^{2}+(4{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}v)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}+4{u}^{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}v+16{u}^{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}v} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}+4{u}^{4}({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}v+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}v)+12{u}^{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}v} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}+4{u}^{4}+12{u}^{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}v} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}(1+{u}^{2}+3{u}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}v)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}+3{u}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}v} \\\\  & =2u\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}+3{u}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sqrt{{u}^{2}}=u$ since $u\\ge 0$ on $D.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{\\color{blue}2u\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}+3{u}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}v}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247100 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1-2u+5v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1-2u+5v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}-5,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}-5,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{(-5)}^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+25+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that the region ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 2$ corresponds to the disk\n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nof radius $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{30}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247141 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-5,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨-5,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}3⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+1+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that the region ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 1$ corresponds to the disk\n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nof radius $1.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{35}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35}\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi \\sqrt{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186253570 "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta })+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =1+({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta })+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =2.\n$$"] [17592186253571 "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{5}\\theta ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta =1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta ) & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta \\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{5}\\theta ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186253572 "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186253574 "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}\\theta\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta =1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot 1+\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186253576 "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }=\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\cdot 1+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{csc}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{csc}\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186253577 "First, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{5}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }=\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta \\cdot ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta \\cdot ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )} & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta \\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{5}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cot}\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186253578 "First, we factor $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ from the first two terms of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x+\\operatorname{sin}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}x+1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)+1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)+1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot 1+1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x+1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}+\\frac{1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}x}+\\frac{1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{sin}x}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{sin}x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}x}+\\frac{1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}x}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}x+{\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x+\\operatorname{sin}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}x+1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}=\\operatorname{cot}x+{\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}x.\n$$"] [17592186253594 "First, we rewrite the first term using the definition of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x}-\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}x)\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x-(\\operatorname{cos}x-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x-\\operatorname{cos}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x-\\operatorname{cos}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x} & =\\frac{({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)-\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$"] [17592186253596 "We put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }+\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}\\theta \\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186253599 "We put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x}+\\operatorname{sin}x & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{1(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x} & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)+\\operatorname{sin}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{2+\\operatorname{sin}x}+\\operatorname{sin}x=1.\n$$"] [17592186253600 "We put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{1}+\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{1+{sin}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}{1\\cdot (1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}+\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{1+{sin}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }+\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta } & =\\frac{1+1}{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n1+\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }=\\frac{2}{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }.\n$$"] [17592186253602 "We put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}x-\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{1}-\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x(1-\\operatorname{sin}x)}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x}-\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x(1-\\operatorname{sin}x)-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x(1-\\operatorname{sin}x)-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x-({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x-1}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(1-\\operatorname{sin}x)}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}x-\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x}=-1.\n$$"] [17592186256656 "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n3x=y\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{t}{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\frac{t}{3},\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 9\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 9$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{t}{3}\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{10}{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=9{x}^{2}y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =9{\\left(\\frac{t}{3}\\right)}^{2}(t) \\\\  & =9\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{9}\\right)(t) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}9{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{9}{\\color{blue}{t}^{3}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186256657 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}1\\le t\\le {e}^{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le {e}^{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1+{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{|t|} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)={e}^{x}{y}^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}t}){t}^{2} \\\\  & =(t){t}^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186256667 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}1\\le t\\le \\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le \\sqrt{6}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{12x}{y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{12{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{12x}{y}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=4{t}^{2}+1,$ $\\text{d}u=8t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =12{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{5}^{25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{25}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{5}^{3}} \\\\  & =125-5\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =5(25-\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186256674 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{2}{t}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}1\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2}{t}\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{4}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}+4}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=x{y}^{6},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{2}{t}\\cdot {t}^{6} \\\\  & =2{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{6}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2{t}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{4}+4,$ $\\text{d}u=4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{5}^{20}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{20}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{5}^{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\sqrt{{20}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{5}^{3}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{40\\sqrt{5}-5\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{35\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186256676 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186256678 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{2x-1}-\\sqrt{4y+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{2x-1}-\\sqrt{4y+1}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186256679 "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ component) is positive for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [17592186256680 "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the vertical direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [17592186256681 "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [17592186256682 "We notice the following:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the vertical direction, our vectors' horizontal components (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}.$"] [17592186256683 "We notice the following:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ component) is zero for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ component) is positive for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=\\mathbf{i}.$"] [17592186256684 "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ component) is zero for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ component) is negative for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=-5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [17592186256686 "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{K}(x,y,z)=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}.$"] [17592186256688 "The function $\\mathbf{F}$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y),\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx-y>0\\,⟹\\,x>y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}x>y\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}(x,y)\\ne (0,0)\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}x>y\\}\\hspace{0.167em}.\n$$"] [17592186256689 "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $y$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y,z)=4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $y$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{j}$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{K}(x,y,z)=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{j}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf{j}.$"] [17592186256690 "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{G}(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $z$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{k}$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{k}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{K}(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram doesn't correspond to any of the listed vector fields."] [17592186256691 "The function $\\mathbf{F}$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or equal to zero. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/9f/9f5f11a4ca31cb796f5a95a1541aa946e24fd1b7079ab8069e5743d41d92d764.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is the entire plane ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ with the origin removed:\n\n![](images/5b/5b77ad4e9574e94e6a05eee7680f4b57d0190d40583c37c2097acef958cab7e2.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/95/95e8d137f90135de1727451fbc96ddfcc325e9e4461054615c91743a4a9ea54a.png)"]}
